Z||000005||rockcrawl||12-25-2000||10:29 PM||||That's it, the new springs and a four wheel alighnment is all you need. The Eibach springs only drop the car .5 to .8 inch I think. Heres a car with the Suspension Techniques springs from JC Whitney. I made the mistake of putting 195/50/15 tires on it. They were way too small. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87gt2_1.jpg"> <BR>same car after new paint<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87gt_2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||MooCow||12-26-2000||12:22 AM||||This is what I am getting if I win.Complete set of "EIBACH" lowering springs for front and rear of Pontiac Fiero. Never installed, in "brand new condition". Lowers car approx. 1 1/2 to 2 inches. For years "84 thru 87".  <P>The person wants $177.50 so far.  Is that a good price or should I wait for another one to come along?  How high should I go?<P>  Thanks for  all the posts and pics so far.<P>                               Thanks Ken<BR>||||reg||1
